# Deterministic line-ending policy (issue #470).
#
# Without this file, a checkout's line endings depend on the cloning machine's local
# `core.autocrlf` setting: with `core.autocrlf=true` (a common Windows default), Git
# rewrites this repository's LF-stored text files to CRLF on checkout even though the
# committed blobs never changed. Repository tooling that parses checked-out files with
# LF-anchored expressions (e.g. tools/review-watch/stage2-report.test.mjs's
# extractCanonicalSkeletonFence, which extracts fenced examples from
# docs/stage2-audit-contract.md with a `\n`-anchored regex) then behaves differently
# purely because of local Git configuration, not because of any real change to
# repository content -- see docs/eol-policy-proof-runs.md for the reproduction and fix.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git's own text/binary heuristic decide what this rule applies to.
# `eol=lf` forces LF on checkout for whatever it classifies as text, regardless of
# `core.autocrlf`. Every currently tracked file is text and every committed blob is
# already LF-only (verified directly via `git cat-file`), so this rule normalizes
# nothing and does not rewrite any existing committed content.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit safety net for common binary types that may be added to this repository
# later: never let Git's text heuristic, this policy, or a future contributor's
# line-ending tooling touch these. None of these extensions are tracked today.
*.png -text
*.jpg -text
*.jpeg -text
*.gif -text
*.ico -text
*.pdf -text
*.zip -text
*.woff -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf -text
*.eot -text
